Here's a simple C code snippet that converts the `argv` array to a single C-style string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *str = (char *)malloc(argc * sizeof(char *));

    for (int i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        strcat(str, argv[i]);
        if (i != argc - 1) {
            strcat(str, " ");
        }
    }

    printf("Converted string: %s\n", str);

    free(str);
    return 0;
}
```

This code uses the `malloc()` function to allocate memory for the resulting string, and then loops through the `argv` array, concatenating each element with a space character (unless it's the last element). Finally, it prints the converted string and frees the allocated memory using `free()`.

