Here's a decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the NODE_ENV variable and calls next() if it's equal to 'true', but returns false if it's equal to 'false':

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';
import { ExecutionContext, Inject } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvMiddleware {
  constructor(private readonly context: ExecutionContext) {}

  async use(context: ExecutionContext) {
    const req = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv === 'true') {
      await this.context.switchToHttp().next();
    } else {
      return false;
    }
  }
}
```

To use this decorator, you can add it to your controller or route like this:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvMiddleware } from './env.middleware';

@Controller('api/v1')
export class MyController {
  @Get()
  @UseMiddleware(new EnvMiddleware())
  getData() {
    // Your code here
  }
}
```

This way, when you access the `getData` endpoint, the `EnvMiddleware` will be executed first. If the NODE_ENV variable is equal to 'true', the request will be forwarded to the next middleware or controller. Otherwise, the response will be false.

