To configure the boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage in your Chrome extension, follow these steps:

1. Create a new file named `inject.js` in your Chrome extension's folder.

2. Open the `manifest.json` file and add the following code to the "content_scripts" section:

```json
{
  "matches": ["https://www.example.com/*"],
  "js": ["inject.js"]
}
```

Replace `https://www.example.com/*` with the URL of the website you want to inject the JavaScript into.

3. In the `inject.js` file, add the following code to inject the JavaScript:

```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function() {
  const script = document.createElement("script");
  script.src = "your-javascript-file.js";
  script.async = true;
  document.body.appendChild(script);
});
```

Replace `your-javascript-file.js` with the name of your JavaScript file that contains the code you want to inject.

4. Save both files and refresh your Chrome browser to test the extension. The JavaScript file should now be injected into the specified webpage.

Remember to replace the URL in the `manifest.json` file with the actual URL of the website you want to inject the JavaScript into. Also, make sure to replace `your-javascript-file.js` with the name of your actual JavaScript file.

