To prevent your planets from being too close together, you can introduce a minimum distance between them during the generation process. You can modify the `generateRandomPlanets` function to check for collisions and adjust the planet's position until it meets the minimum distance requirement. Here's an updated version of the function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    } else {
      // Adjust the planet's position until it meets the minimum distance requirement
      do {
        planet.position = [
          randomPosition(-30, 30),
          randomPosition(-30, 30),
          randomPosition(-30, 30),
        ];

        tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
          p.position.some((coord, index) => {
            return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
          })
        );
      } while (tooClose);

      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

Make sure to update the `minDistance` variable to the desired value, which will determine the minimum distance between the planets.

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows from text by adjusting the line breaks. This utility uses the `react-syntax-highlighter` library for syntax highlighting.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { SyntaxHighlighter } from 'react-syntax-highlighter';
import { atomOneDark } from 'react-syntax-highlighter/dist/cjs/styles/atomOneDark';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [widowedText, setWidowedText] = useState(text);

  const handleRemoveWidows = () => {
    const lines = widowedText.split('\n');
    const adjustedLines = lines.map((line) => {
      const words = line.split(' ');
      const lastWordIndex = words.length - 1;

      if (words[lastWordIndex].length > 0 && words[lastWordIndex - 1].length === 0) {
        words.pop();
      }

      return words.join(' ');
    });

    setWidowedText(adjustedLines.join('\n'));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <SyntaxHighlighter language="javascript" style={atomOneDark}>
        {widowedText}
      </SyntaxHighlighter>
      <button onClick={handleRemoveWidows}>Remove Widows</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this component, simply pass in the text you want to remove widows from:

```jsx
<RemoveWidows text="This is a sample text with some widows." />
```

This component will display the input text with syntax highlighting and a button to remove widows. When you click the button, the text will be adjusted to remove any widows.

