Here's a PowerShell script that recursively checks folders for ACLs referring to SIDs found only in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain:

```powershell
# Define function to check ACLs for SIDHistory references
function CheckACLs($path) {
    $acl = Get-Acl -Path $path
    foreach ($access in $acl.Access) {
        if ($access.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21-(\d+)-(\d+)-(\d+)-(\d+)$') {
            $sid = New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier($access.IdentityReference)
            if ((Get-ADUser -Filter "SidHistory -eq '$sid'" -Properties SIDHistory).Count -eq 0) {
                Write-Host "Potential issue found in $path. ACL refers to SID not present in domain users or groups."
            }
        }
    }
}

# Define root folder to start checking
$rootFolder = "C:\Your_Root_Folder"

# Recursively check folders and subfolders
Get-ChildItem -Path $rootFolder -Directory | ForEach-Object {
    CheckACLs $_
    Get-ChildItem -Path $_.FullName -Directory | ForEach-Object {
        CheckACLs $_
    }
}
```

Replace `C:\Your_Root_Folder` with the root folder you want to start checking. This script will recursively check all folders and subfolders under the specified root folder for ACLs referring to SIDs not present in the domain users or groups.
